    "['ip route-static [destination_ip] [destination_mask] [next_hop]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip route 3.3.3.3 255.255.255.255 2.1.1.2', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nip route 3.3.3.3 255.255.255.255 2.1.1.2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei `ip route-static` command is equivalent to Cisco's `ip route` command. Both create static routes with the same parameters:\\n- Destination network: 3.3.3.3\\n- Mask: 255.255.255.255 (host route)\\n- Next-hop: 2.1.1.2\\n\\nThe syntax is nearly identical between the two vendors for basic static routes, with only the command keyword differing (`route-static` vs `route`).\", 'trans_template': 'ip route 3.3.3.3 255.255.255.255 2.1.1.2'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-policy [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel policy tpatoc', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'tunnel policy tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for the Huawei `tunnel-policy` configuration:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel policy tpatoc\\ntunnel destination 2.2.2.2 binding Tunnel10\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco uses \"tunnel policy\" instead of \"tunnel-policy\" as the command syntax\\n2. The functionality remains the same - binding the tunnel policy to destination 2.2.2.2 with TE tunnel interface Tunnel10\\n3. The Cisco command maintains the same policy name \"tpatoc\" for consistency', 'trans_template': 'tunnel policy tpatoc'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel destination 2.2.2.2 binding Tunnel10', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'tunnel destination 2.2.2.2 binding Tunnel10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for the Huawei `tunnel-policy` configuration:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel policy tpatoc\\ntunnel destination 2.2.2.2 binding Tunnel10\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco uses \"tunnel policy\" instead of \"tunnel-policy\" as the command syntax\\n2. The functionality remains the same - binding the tunnel policy to destination 2.2.2.2 with TE tunnel interface Tunnel10\\n3. The Cisco command maintains the same policy name \"tpatoc\" for consistency', 'trans_template': 'tunnel destination 2.2.2.2 binding Tunnel10'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel binding destination [parameter1] te [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng binding sid 2.2.2.2 type tunnel-te tunnel-id 100', 'parent_command': ['tunnel policy tpatoc'], 'root': 'tunnel policy tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the HUAWEI `tunnel binding` configuration:\\n\\n##\\ntunnel mpls traffic-eng binding sid 2.2.2.2 type tunnel-te tunnel-id 100\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI command binds destination 2.2.2.2 to TE tunnel Tunnel10\\n- In Cisco, we use the `tunnel mpls traffic-eng binding` command to achieve similar functionality\\n- The `sid` parameter specifies the destination address (2.2.2.2)\\n- `type tunnel-te` specifies it's a TE tunnel\\n- `tunnel-id 100` matches the tunnel ID from the HUAWEI configuration (seen in the Tunnel10 interface configuration)\\n- Note that Cisco typically uses tunnel interface numbers rather than names, so we reference the ID directly\", 'trans_template': 'tunnel mpls traffic-eng binding sid 2.2.2.2 type tunnel-te tunnel-id 100'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls-tp meg [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls tp meg test', 'parent_command': ['tunnel policy tpatoc'], 'root': 'tunnel policy tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for the HUAWEI `mpls-tp meg test` command:\\n\\n##\\nmpls tp meg test\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco command structure is very similar for MPLS-TP MEG configuration\\n2. The actual functionality may differ slightly between vendors in implementation details\\n3. Additional parameters would need to be configured under this meg context in Cisco, similar to the HUAWEI subcommands shown in the original configuration\", 'trans_template': 'mpls tp meg test'}]]": 1,
    "['me l2vc peer-ip [peer_ip] vc-id [vc_id] vc-type [vc_type] mep-id [mep_id] remote-mep-id [remote_mep_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls oam l2vc peer 3.3.3.3 vcid 30000 vlan mep 1 remote-mep 2', 'parent_command': ['tunnel policy tpatoc', 'mpls tp meg test'], 'root': 'tunnel policy tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Huawei MPLS-TP MEP configuration:\\n\\n##\\nmpls oam l2vc peer 3.3.3.3 vcid 30000 vlan mep 1 remote-mep 2\\n##\\n\\nNote: This translation assumes Cisco IOS XE or similar platform with MPLS-TP support. The key differences are:\\n1. \"me l2vc\" becomes \"mpls oam l2vc\"\\n2. \"peer-ip\" becomes \"peer\"\\n3. \"vc-id\" becomes \"vcid\"\\n4. \"mep-id\" becomes \"mep\"\\n5. \"remote-mep-id\" becomes \"remote-mep\"', 'trans_template': 'mpls oam l2vc peer 3.3.3.3 vcid 30000 vlan mep 1 remote-mep 2'}]]": 1,
    "['cc send [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls oam cc send', 'parent_command': ['tunnel policy tpatoc', 'mpls tp meg test'], 'root': 'tunnel policy tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nmpls oam cc send\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Huawei, `cc send enable` activates Continuity Check (CC) message sending in MPLS-TP OAM.\\n- The equivalent Cisco command is `mpls oam cc send`, which enables the transmission of CC messages for MPLS OAM.\\n- Both commands serve the same purpose of enabling continuity check message transmission for MPLS OAM/TP monitoring.', 'trans_template': 'mpls oam cc send'}]]": 1,
    "['cc receive enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls oam cc receive', 'parent_command': ['tunnel policy tpatoc', 'mpls tp meg test'], 'root': 'tunnel policy tpatoc', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nmpls oam cc receive\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nIn Cisco devices, the equivalent command to enable Continuity Check (CC) reception in MPLS-TP is `mpls oam cc receive`. This command enables the MPLS OAM Continuity Check mechanism for receiving packets, which serves the same purpose as Huawei's `cc receive enable` in the MPLS-TP MEG (Maintenance Entity Group) context.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls oam cc receive'}]]": 1,
    "['lost-measure dual-ended [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls tp loss-measurement dual-ended', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'mpls tp loss-measurement dual-ended', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Based on the HUAWEI command `lost-measure dual-ended enable`, which enables dual-ended loss measurement for MPLS-TP (MPLS Transport Profile) connectivity verification, the equivalent Cisco command would be:\\n\\n##\\nmpls tp loss-measurement dual-ended\\n##\\n\\nThis Cisco command enables bidirectional (dual-ended) loss measurement for MPLS-TP, which is the equivalent functionality to HUAWEI's `lost-measure dual-ended enable`.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls tp loss-measurement dual-ended'}]]": 1,
    "['[parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'end', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'end', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nend\\n##\\n\\nThe `return` command in Huawei configuration exits the current view and returns to the user view (similar to exiting configuration mode in Cisco). The equivalent Cisco command is `end`, which exits global configuration mode and returns to privileged EXEC mode.', 'trans_template': 'end'}]]": 1
